    private val additionalBookEntries = listOf(
        StudyEntry("Leviticus", "Holiness, worship and community", "Books", "Leviticus gives Israel instructions about offerings, priesthood, purity, festivals and ethical life. Its central concern is that the people live as a community set apart for God.", listOf("Leviticus 1", "Leviticus 16", "Leviticus 19")),
        StudyEntry("Numbers", "Wilderness journeys and faithfulness", "Books", "Numbers follows Israel from Sinai through the wilderness toward the promised land. It records censuses, rebellion, leadership challenges and God's continued provision.", listOf("Numbers 6", "Numbers 14", "Numbers 21")),
        StudyEntry("Deuteronomy", "Remembering the covenant", "Books", "Deuteronomy presents Moses' final speeches to Israel before entering the land. It calls the people to remember God's acts, love God wholeheartedly and remain faithful to the covenant.", listOf("Deuteronomy 6", "Deuteronomy 8", "Deuteronomy 30")),
        StudyEntry("Joshua", "Entering the promised land", "Books", "Joshua describes Israel's entry into Canaan, the battles associated with the conquest and the distribution of the land among the tribes. It closes with a call to choose whom the people will serve.", listOf("Joshua 1", "Joshua 6", "Joshua 24")),
        StudyEntry("Judges", "A repeated cycle of failure and rescue", "Books", "Judges describes the period before Israel's monarchy. The people repeatedly turn away, face oppression, cry out and are delivered through judges; the cycle exposes the cost of unfaithfulness and disunity.", listOf("Judges 2", "Judges 6", "Judges 21")),
        StudyEntry("Ruth", "Loyalty, kindness and redemption", "Books", "Ruth tells how a Moabite widow remains loyal to Naomi and becomes part of the family line of David. The story highlights steadfast love, providence and welcome across social boundaries.", listOf("Ruth 1", "Ruth 2", "Ruth 4")),
        StudyEntry("1 Samuel", "Samuel, Saul and the rise of David", "Books", "First Samuel moves from the ministry of Samuel to Israel's first king, Saul, and the emergence of David. It explores leadership, obedience, fear and the difference between outward appearance and the heart.", listOf("1 Samuel 3", "1 Samuel 8", "1 Samuel 16", "1 Samuel 17")),
        StudyEntry("2 Samuel", "David's reign and its consequences", "Books", "Second Samuel focuses on David's kingship, the covenant promise made to him, his failures and the turmoil within his family. It presents both the significance and the limits of human kingship.", listOf("2 Samuel 7", "2 Samuel 11", "2 Samuel 12")),
        StudyEntry("1 Kings", "Solomon and the divided kingdom", "Books", "First Kings begins with Solomon's reign and the building of the Temple, then traces the division of the kingdom and the ministries of prophets including Elijah. It contrasts wisdom and worship with idolatry and injustice.", listOf("1 Kings 3", "1 Kings 8", "1 Kings 18")),
        StudyEntry("2 Kings", "The decline and exile of Israel and Judah", "Books", "Second Kings follows the kingdoms through political instability, prophetic warnings and eventual exile. The narrative repeatedly evaluates rulers by their faithfulness to God and the covenant.", listOf("2 Kings 17", "2 Kings 22", "2 Kings 25")),
        StudyEntry("1 Chronicles", "Israel's story and David's worship", "Books", "First Chronicles retells Israel's story with special attention to genealogies, David's reign and preparations for Temple worship. It emphasizes continuity, worship and the responsibilities of leadership.", listOf("1 Chronicles 16", "1 Chronicles 17", "1 Chronicles 29")),
        StudyEntry("2 Chronicles", "Judah, the Temple and reform", "Books", "Second Chronicles focuses on Solomon and the kings of Judah, highlighting the Temple, worship and periods of reform. It ends with the exile and the decree allowing a return.", listOf("2 Chronicles 7", "2 Chronicles 20", "2 Chronicles 36")),
        StudyEntry("Ezra", "Return from exile and restoration", "Books", "Ezra describes groups returning from Babylon, rebuilding the Temple and renewing commitment to the law. Restoration involves both rebuilding a place of worship and reshaping communal life.", listOf("Ezra 1", "Ezra 3", "Ezra 7")),
        StudyEntry("Nehemiah", "Rebuilding Jerusalem's walls", "Books", "Nehemiah recounts the rebuilding of Jerusalem's walls despite opposition, followed by public reading of the law and communal reform. Prayer, practical leadership and shared responsibility run throughout.", listOf("Nehemiah 1", "Nehemiah 4", "Nehemiah 8")),
        StudyEntry("Esther", "Courage and providence in exile", "Books", "Esther tells how a Jewish woman becomes queen in Persia and risks her position to protect her people from destruction. God's name is not directly mentioned, but providence is a central theme.", listOf("Esther 4", "Esther 7", "Esther 9")),
        StudyEntry("Job", "Suffering, wisdom and honest questions", "Books", "Job explores the suffering of a righteous man and challenges simplistic explanations that equate hardship with personal wrongdoing. Its dialogues make room for lament, humility and the limits of human understanding.", listOf("Job 1", "Job 19", "Job 38", "Job 42")),
        StudyEntry("Proverbs", "Wisdom for everyday life", "Books", "Proverbs gathers short teachings about speech, work, relationships, justice, money and self-control. Wisdom begins with reverence for God and is expressed through practical choices.", listOf("Proverbs 1", "Proverbs 3", "Proverbs 8", "Proverbs 31")),
        StudyEntry("Ecclesiastes", "Meaning, limits and the gift of life", "Books", "Ecclesiastes reflects on the limits of human achievement and the fleeting nature of life. It urges readers to recognize their limits, receive ordinary gifts with gratitude and live responsibly before God.", listOf("Ecclesiastes 1", "Ecclesiastes 3", "Ecclesiastes 12")),
        StudyEntry("Song of Solomon", "Love poetry", "Books", "Song of Solomon is a collection of love poems celebrating desire, affection and commitment. Its poetic imagery should be read as poetry rather than as a sequence of literal historical events.", listOf("Song of Solomon 2", "Song of Solomon 4", "Song of Solomon 8")),
        StudyEntry("Isaiah", "Judgment, hope and the Holy One", "Books", "Isaiah addresses Judah and the nations with warnings about injustice and misplaced trust, alongside promises of restoration and hope. Its visions of a coming king and servant have been especially important in Christian reading.", listOf("Isaiah 6", "Isaiah 9", "Isaiah 40", "Isaiah 53")),
        StudyEntry("Jeremiah", "Warning before Jerusalem's fall", "Books", "Jeremiah calls Judah to turn from idolatry and injustice as the Babylonian crisis approaches. Alongside judgment, the book promises restoration and a new covenant written on the heart.", listOf("Jeremiah 1", "Jeremiah 7", "Jeremiah 29", "Jeremiah 31")),
        StudyEntry("Lamentations", "Grief after Jerusalem's destruction", "Books", "Lamentations gives poetic expression to the devastation of Jerusalem. It does not hide grief, yet it also remembers God's mercy and the possibility of hope.", listOf("Lamentations 1", "Lamentations 3", "Lamentations 5")),
        StudyEntry("Ezekiel", "Visions, accountability and restoration", "Books", "Ezekiel speaks to exiles through vivid visions and symbolic actions. The book addresses responsibility, the departure and return of God's glory, and the promise of a renewed people.", listOf("Ezekiel 1", "Ezekiel 18", "Ezekiel 36", "Ezekiel 37")),
        StudyEntry("Daniel", "Faithfulness under foreign empires", "Books", "Daniel combines stories of faithful Jews in foreign courts with visions of kingdoms and God's ultimate rule. It encourages steadfastness when political powers demand ultimate loyalty.", listOf("Daniel 1", "Daniel 3", "Daniel 6", "Daniel 7")),
        StudyEntry("Hosea", "Covenant love and unfaithfulness", "Books", "Hosea uses the prophet's troubled marriage as a sign of Israel's unfaithfulness and God's persistent commitment. The book calls the people back to knowing God rather than relying on empty ritual.", listOf("Hosea 2", "Hosea 6", "Hosea 11")),
        StudyEntry("Joel", "The day of the LORD and renewal", "Books", "Joel interprets a devastating locust plague as a call to repentance and speaks of the day of the LORD. It also promises the outpouring of God's Spirit, a passage later quoted in Acts.", listOf("Joel 2", "Joel 3", "Acts 2")),
        StudyEntry("Amos", "Justice and genuine worship", "Books", "Amos condemns exploitation, corruption and religious hypocrisy in a prosperous society. The prophet insists that worship cannot be separated from justice and righteousness.", listOf("Amos 3", "Amos 5", "Amos 9")),
        StudyEntry("Obadiah", "Pride and accountability", "Books", "Obadiah is a short prophecy against Edom for its pride and hostility toward Judah during Jerusalem's crisis. It announces that nations are accountable for how they treat others.", listOf("Obadiah 1")),
        StudyEntry("Jonah", "Mercy beyond expected boundaries", "Books", "Jonah tells of a reluctant prophet sent to Nineveh. The story challenges narrow ideas of who deserves mercy and exposes the prophet's resistance to God's compassion.", listOf("Jonah 1", "Jonah 3", "Jonah 4")),
        StudyEntry("Micah", "Justice, mercy and humble faithfulness", "Books", "Micah confronts corrupt leaders and false security while promising restoration. Its best-known summary calls for justice, mercy and humble walking with God.", listOf("Micah 4", "Micah 5", "Micah 6")),
        StudyEntry("Nahum", "The fall of Nineveh", "Books", "Nahum announces the downfall of Nineveh, the capital of Assyria. Its message speaks to people oppressed by imperial violence and declares that cruelty will not last forever.", listOf("Nahum 1", "Nahum 3")),
        StudyEntry("Habakkuk", "Faith amid injustice", "Books", "Habakkuk questions why injustice goes unanswered and struggles with the use of Babylon as an instrument of judgment. The book ends with a confession of trust even when circumstances remain difficult.", listOf("Habakkuk 1", "Habakkuk 2", "Habakkuk 3")),
        StudyEntry("Zephaniah", "Judgment and a purified people", "Books", "Zephaniah warns of the day of the LORD and calls for humility and repentance. It also looks toward a restored community that rejoices in God's presence.", listOf("Zephaniah 1", "Zephaniah 2", "Zephaniah 3")),
        StudyEntry("Haggai", "Rebuilding the Temple", "Books", "Haggai challenges the returned exiles to reconsider their priorities and resume rebuilding the Temple. The book links renewed worship with courage and hope for the future.", listOf("Haggai 1", "Haggai 2")),
        StudyEntry("Zechariah", "Visions of restoration", "Books", "Zechariah encourages the post-exilic community through symbolic visions and promises of God's return to Jerusalem. It looks toward purification, a coming king and a renewed future.", listOf("Zechariah 3", "Zechariah 9", "Zechariah 12", "Zechariah 14")),
        StudyEntry("Malachi", "Covenant faithfulness and expectation", "Books", "Malachi challenges careless worship, injustice and unfaithfulness. It closes the Old Testament prophetic collection with expectation of a messenger who prepares the way.", listOf("Malachi 1", "Malachi 3", "Malachi 4")),
        StudyEntry("1 Corinthians", "A church learning to live as one body", "Books", "First Corinthians addresses divisions, moral disorder, worship, spiritual gifts and resurrection. Paul repeatedly calls the church to unity shaped by the cross and love.", listOf("1 Corinthians 1", "1 Corinthians 13", "1 Corinthians 15")),
        StudyEntry("2 Corinthians", "Weakness, ministry and reconciliation", "Books", "Second Corinthians defends Paul's ministry while explaining the paradox of strength through weakness. It also emphasizes reconciliation, generosity and integrity in Christian service.", listOf("2 Corinthians 4", "2 Corinthians 5", "2 Corinthians 8", "2 Corinthians 12")),
        StudyEntry("Galatians", "Freedom through faith in Christ", "Books", "Galatians argues that Gentile believers are not required to take on the markers of the Jewish law to belong to God's people. Christian freedom is expressed through faith working in love and life shaped by the Spirit.", listOf("Galatians 2", "Galatians 3", "Galatians 5")),
        StudyEntry("Ephesians", "The church united in Christ", "Books", "Ephesians describes God's purpose to unite all things in Christ and form one people from different backgrounds. It moves from identity and grace to practical unity, integrity and spiritual resilience.", listOf("Ephesians 1", "Ephesians 2", "Ephesians 4", "Ephesians 6")),
        StudyEntry("Philippians", "Joy, humility and perseverance", "Books", "Philippians is a warm letter written from imprisonment. It encourages joy, partnership in the gospel, humility modeled by Christ and perseverance in faithful living.", listOf("Philippians 1", "Philippians 2", "Philippians 3", "Philippians 4")),
        StudyEntry("Colossians", "The supremacy of Christ", "Books", "Colossians emphasizes the centrality and supremacy of Christ over creation and the church. It calls believers to set their minds on Christ and let that identity shape relationships and conduct.", listOf("Colossians 1", "Colossians 2", "Colossians 3")),
        StudyEntry("1 Thessalonians", "Hope and encouragement", "Books", "First Thessalonians encourages a young church facing pressure. It highlights faith, love, hope, holy living and comfort concerning believers who have died.", listOf("1 Thessalonians 1", "1 Thessalonians 4", "1 Thessalonians 5")),
        StudyEntry("2 Thessalonians", "Steadfastness and the day of the Lord", "Books", "Second Thessalonians encourages believers not to be unsettled by claims about the day of the Lord. It calls for endurance, responsible work and confidence in God's justice.", listOf("2 Thessalonians 1", "2 Thessalonians 2", "2 Thessalonians 3")),
        StudyEntry("1 Timothy", "Guidance for church life", "Books", "First Timothy gives instructions about teaching, worship, leadership and care within a Christian community. It stresses sound teaching, integrity and practical care for people.", listOf("1 Timothy 3", "1 Timothy 4", "1 Timothy 6")),
        StudyEntry("2 Timothy", "Perseverance in the gospel", "Books", "Second Timothy urges faithfulness in teaching and ministry despite hardship. It emphasizes Scripture, endurance and passing the message on to others.", listOf("2 Timothy 1", "2 Timothy 3", "2 Timothy 4")),
        StudyEntry("Titus", "Healthy teaching and good works", "Books", "Titus offers guidance for appointing leaders and building communities marked by self-control, integrity and good works. It connects God's grace with a transformed way of life.", listOf("Titus 1", "Titus 2", "Titus 3")),
        StudyEntry("Philemon", "A request shaped by fellowship", "Books", "Philemon is a personal letter in which Paul appeals for Onesimus to be welcomed not merely as a servant but as a beloved brother. It shows how the gospel reshapes relationships and status.", listOf("Philemon 1")),
        StudyEntry("Hebrews", "Jesus, the great high priest", "Books", "Hebrews presents Jesus as greater than the old covenant's angels, Moses and priesthood, and explains his once-for-all offering. It calls readers to hold fast, encourage one another and persevere in faith.", listOf("Hebrews 1", "Hebrews 4", "Hebrews 10", "Hebrews 11")),
        StudyEntry("James", "Faith made visible in action", "Books", "James addresses practical discipleship: speech, impartiality, care for the poor, wisdom and patience. It insists that genuine faith becomes visible in actions and relationships.", listOf("James 1", "James 2", "James 3", "James 5")),
        StudyEntry("1 Peter", "Hope and holiness under pressure", "Books", "First Peter encourages believers experiencing hardship to live with hope, holiness and honourable conduct. It points to Jesus' suffering as a pattern for faithful endurance.", listOf("1 Peter 1", "1 Peter 2", "1 Peter 3", "1 Peter 5")),
        StudyEntry("2 Peter", "Growth and discernment", "Books", "Second Peter calls believers to grow in character and warns against destructive teaching. It encourages confidence in God's promises and patience concerning the Lord's return.", listOf("2 Peter 1", "2 Peter 2", "2 Peter 3")),
        StudyEntry("1 John", "Truth, love and assurance", "Books", "First John tests claims about knowing God by their relationship to truth, obedience and love. It encourages assurance while warning against deception and hatred.", listOf("1 John 1", "1 John 3", "1 John 4", "1 John 5")),
        StudyEntry("2 John", "Walking in truth and love", "Books", "Second John is a brief letter urging a community to remain in the truth and love one another. It also warns against welcoming teaching that distorts the identity of Jesus.", listOf("2 John 1")),
        StudyEntry("3 John", "Hospitality and faithful leadership", "Books", "Third John commends hospitality toward travelling Christian workers and criticizes a leader who rejects them. It highlights the importance of truth expressed through generous action.", listOf("3 John 1")),
        StudyEntry("Jude", "Contending for the faith", "Books", "Jude warns against teachers whose conduct contradicts their claims and urges believers to remain grounded in faith, prayer and mercy. It closes with a declaration of God's power to keep his people.", listOf("Jude 1")),
        StudyEntry("Revelation", "Faithfulness and the renewal of all things", "Books", "Revelation uses visions, symbols and worship scenes to encourage churches facing pressure. It portrays the victory of God and the Lamb, calls for faithful witness and ends with the vision of a renewed creation.", listOf("Revelation 1", "Revelation 5", "Revelation 21", "Revelation 22"))
    )

    val entries = listOf(
        StudyEntry("Genesis", "Beginnings, covenant and promise", "Books", "Genesis introduces creation, humanity, the fall, the flood and the beginnings of Israel through Abraham, Isaac, Jacob and Joseph. Its major movements are creation (1–2), rebellion and its consequences (3–11), and the family stories of the patriarchs (12–50).", listOf("Genesis 1", "Genesis 12", "Genesis 15", "Genesis 37")),
        StudyEntry("Exodus", "Rescue, covenant and worship", "Books", "Exodus tells how God delivers Israel from slavery in Egypt, forms the people through the covenant at Sinai, and gives instructions for the tabernacle. Watch the movement from oppression to deliverance, covenant and God's presence among the people.", listOf("Exodus 3", "Exodus 12", "Exodus 19", "Exodus 20")),
        StudyEntry("Psalms", "Prayer, praise and lament", "Books", "Psalms is a collection of prayers and songs. It gives language for praise, thanksgiving, grief, confession, trust and hope. Notice how many psalms move from honest trouble toward renewed trust in God.", listOf("Psalm 1", "Psalm 23", "Psalm 51", "Psalm 139")),
        StudyEntry("Matthew", "Jesus the Messiah and the kingdom", "Books", "Matthew presents Jesus as the promised Messiah and teacher. It frequently connects Jesus' life and ministry with Israel's Scriptures. The Sermon on the Mount (chapters 5–7), parables of the kingdom and the passion narrative are central sections.", listOf("Matthew 5", "Matthew 13", "Matthew 16", "Matthew 28")),
        StudyEntry("Mark", "The actions and authority of Jesus", "Books", "Mark is a fast-moving account of Jesus' ministry. It emphasizes his authority, compassion, teaching, suffering and call to discipleship. The question of who Jesus is runs through the whole book.", listOf("Mark 1", "Mark 4", "Mark 8", "Mark 15")),
        StudyEntry("Luke", "Good news for all people", "Books", "Luke gives an orderly account of Jesus' life and highlights prayer, the Holy Spirit, mercy and God's concern for people often overlooked. Its parables and encounters invite readers to consider repentance, generosity and neighbour-love.", listOf("Luke 1", "Luke 10", "Luke 15", "Luke 24")),
        StudyEntry("John", "Signs, belief and life in Christ", "Books", "John explicitly states that its purpose is to lead readers to believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and to have life in his name. Follow the signs, conversations and 'I am' sayings, and notice the themes of life, light, truth and love.", listOf("John 1", "John 3", "John 15", "John 20")),
        StudyEntry("Acts", "The early church and its mission", "Books", "Acts traces the spread of the Christian movement from Jerusalem outward. The Holy Spirit empowers witness, the gospel crosses cultural boundaries, and communities learn to live out their faith together.", listOf("Acts 2", "Acts 9", "Acts 10", "Acts 15")),
        StudyEntry("Romans", "The gospel and life shaped by grace", "Books", "Romans explains the universal problem of sin, God's righteousness and the saving work of Jesus. It then turns to the practical shape of a life offered to God, marked by humility, love and service.", listOf("Romans 3", "Romans 5", "Romans 8", "Romans 12")),
        StudyEntry("David", "Shepherd, king and psalmist", "People", "David moves from shepherd to king and is remembered for courage, leadership, worship and serious moral failure. His story shows both the importance of trusting God and the consequences of abusing power. Read his life with both honesty and care.", listOf("1 Samuel 16", "1 Samuel 17", "2 Samuel 7", "2 Samuel 11")),
        StudyEntry("Moses", "Leader of the exodus", "People", "Moses is called to confront Pharaoh and lead Israel out of Egypt. He becomes the mediator of the Sinai covenant and guides the people through the wilderness. His story includes reluctance, perseverance, intercession and dependence on God.", listOf("Exodus 3", "Exodus 14", "Exodus 20", "Deuteronomy 34")),
        StudyEntry("Mary", "The mother of Jesus", "People", "Mary receives the announcement that she will give birth to Jesus. Luke portrays her response with humility and trust, and places a song of praise on her lips. The Gospels also show her present at important moments in Jesus' life.", listOf("Luke 1", "Luke 2", "John 2", "John 19")),
        StudyEntry("Peter", "Disciple, witness and apostle", "People", "Peter is outspoken and sometimes impulsive. He confesses Jesus as the Messiah, fails under pressure, is restored, and becomes a prominent witness in Acts. His story is a study in both human weakness and renewed responsibility.", listOf("Matthew 16", "Luke 22", "John 21", "Acts 2")),
        StudyEntry("Paul", "Apostle to the nations", "People", "Paul changes from persecutor of the church to a missionary and teacher after his encounter with the risen Jesus. Acts and his letters describe his travels, pastoral concerns and teaching about the gospel and life in Christ.", listOf("Acts 9", "Acts 17", "Romans 1", "2 Timothy 4")),
        StudyEntry("Creation and new creation", "A theme across Scripture", "Themes", "The Bible opens with God creating the heavens and the earth and closes with the vision of renewed creation. This theme frames the biblical story: creation, human rebellion, redemption and restoration.", listOf("Genesis 1", "Psalm 104", "Romans 8", "Revelation 21")),
        StudyEntry("Covenant", "God's promises and commitments", "Themes", "Covenant is a major way Scripture describes God's committed relationship with people. Key covenant moments include Noah, Abraham, Israel at Sinai and the new covenant announced by the prophets and Jesus.", listOf("Genesis 9", "Genesis 15", "Exodus 19", "Jeremiah 31", "Luke 22")),
        StudyEntry("Kingdom of God", "God's reign and its values", "Themes", "Jesus announces the kingdom of God as central to his message. His teaching describes its surprising values: humility, mercy, forgiveness, justice, faithful obedience and care for others. The kingdom is both present in Jesus' ministry and awaited in its fullness.", listOf("Matthew 5", "Matthew 13", "Mark 1", "Luke 4")),
        StudyEntry("Prayer", "Speaking and listening before God", "Themes", "Biblical prayer includes praise, confession, requests, lament and thanksgiving. Jesus teaches his disciples to pray with trust and sincerity rather than performance. The Psalms provide a broad vocabulary for prayer in every season.", listOf("Psalm 51", "Matthew 6", "Luke 11", "Philippians 4")),
        StudyEntry("Grace and faith", "The gift of salvation", "Themes", "The New Testament describes salvation as God's gift, received through faith rather than earned as a personal achievement. Grace does not make good works irrelevant; it leads to a changed life marked by love and service.", listOf("John 1", "Romans 3", "Ephesians 2", "Titus 2")),
